[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:11] I don't have weekly traffic, a test-store list, or competitor price moves. Your argument assumes competitors face the same input costs and will follow with price. What if the CEO tells you they've actually held prices, and Brew & Bean's customers are loyal precisely because of that?

[candidate · 13:41] Then I'd say the loyalty is real but it's being bought at 18 points of margin, and that's not sustainable regardless of what competitors do. If they've held prices while facing the same 40% input spike, they're absorbing it too — either they're subsidizing from somewhere else, or they'll move within a few quarters. Being the last to raise isn't a durable position.

I'd also note that customers have already shown they're not purely price-driven: average ticket went from $6.20 to $6.80 on more items per visit, not price. That's a customer willing to spend more in a sitting, which suggests some headroom.

And I'd reframe the ask — it's not price versus loyalty, it's a smaller price step plus supplier renegotiation plus waste reduction, so no single lever has to carry 18 points.

Could I get churn rates, repeat purchase frequency, brand survey data, and the supplier contract renewal calendar? That would let me test the loyalty claim directly.
